Usage Example
// After FeatureConverter produces a flat matrix:
auto converted = convertTensorToArma(tensor, config);

// Build TimeFrameIndex vector from valid row indices
std::vector&lt;TimeFrameIndex&gt; times;
for (auto idx : converted.valid_row_indices) {
    times.emplace_back(static_cast&lt;int64_t&gt;(idx));
}

// Segment for HMM training
auto segments = SequenceAssembler::segment(
    converted.matrix, labels, times,
    {.min_sequence_length = 2});

// Each segment is a contiguous temporal run
for (auto const & seg : segments) {
    // seg.features: features × segment_length
    // seg.labels:   1 × segment_length
    // seg.times:    original time indices
}

Bounding Span

Motivation
Consider training labels on frames 1–1000 and 2000–3000, with a prediction target of 1001–1999:

Naively predicting only frames 1001–1999 loses temporal context at boundaries
Bounding span predicts frames 1–3000 (the full union) and filters output to 1001–1999
The HMM carries state through frame 1000→1001 seamlessly

Since Viterbi complexity is \(O(K^2 \times N)\) where \(K\) = states and \(N\) = frames, this is cheap. For \(K=2\) and \(N=100{,}000\): ~2.4M FLOPs (sub-millisecond).


API
namespace MLCore {

struct BoundingSpan {
    TimeFrameIndex min_time;
    TimeFrameIndex max_time;
};

/// Compute [min(start), max(end)] across all intervals
std::optional&lt;BoundingSpan&gt; computeIntervalBounds(
    DigitalIntervalSeries const & series);

/// Compute [min, max] of a time vector
std::optional&lt;BoundingSpan&gt; computeRowTimeBounds(
    std::vector&lt;TimeFrameIndex&gt; const & times);

/// Union of two spans
BoundingSpan mergeSpans(BoundingSpan a, BoundingSpan b);

/// Keep only feature columns whose time falls within [min, max]
FilteredRows filterRowsToSpan(
    arma::mat const & features,
    std::vector&lt;TimeFrameIndex&gt; const & times,
    BoundingSpan span);

/// Keep only predictions at times inside any interval
FilteredPredictions filterPredictionsToIntervals(
    arma::Row&lt;std::size_t&gt; const & predictions,
    std::optional&lt;arma::mat&gt; const & probabilities,
    std::vector&lt;TimeFrameIndex&gt; const & times,
    DigitalIntervalSeries const & intervals);

} // namespace MLCore


Pipeline Integration
In ClassificationPipeline, bounding span logic executes when prediction_interval_key is non-empty:
Stage 6 (Predict) — input filtering:

Load the prediction DigitalIntervalSeries from DataManager
Compute its bounds; merge with training label interval bounds if available
filterRowsToSpan() restricts predict_features and predict_row_times
Prediction proceeds on the filtered (but temporally complete) range

Stage 8 (Output) — output filtering:

filterPredictionsToIntervals() restricts predictions, probabilities, and times to only frames within the prediction intervals
Filtered output is passed to PredictionWriter

Constrained Viterbi Decoding

Motivation
When the prediction range is adjacent to labeled training regions, the HMM can leverage known ground truth at boundaries. If frame 1000 is labeled as state 1 with 100% certainty, the HMM should start from that state when predicting frame 1001 rather than guessing from the prior distribution.


Implementation
For each prediction segment adjacent to labeled data:

Build a map from TimeFrameIndex → training label
For each segment’s first frame, check if there’s a known label at that frame or at the frame immediately before it
If found, clamp hmm.Initial() to a delta distribution on that state
Run Viterbi, then restore the original initial distribution

// Simplified pipeline logic:
std::unordered_map&lt;int64_t, std::size_t&gt; label_map;
for (std::size_t i = 0; i &lt; train_times.size(); ++i) {
    label_map[train_times[i].getValue()] = train_labels[i];
}

std::vector&lt;std::optional&lt;std::size_t&gt;&gt; constraints;
for (auto const & seg : pred_segments) {
    auto first = seg.times.front().getValue();
    if (auto it = label_map.find(first); it != label_map.end())
        constraints.emplace_back(it-&gt;second);
    else if (auto it2 = label_map.find(first - 1); it2 != label_map.end())
        constraints.emplace_back(it2-&gt;second);
    else
        constraints.emplace_back(std::nullopt);
}

model-&gt;predictSequencesConstrained(feature_seqs, pred_seqs, constraints);


Scope

Applies only to sequence models (isSequenceModel() == true)
Gated by config.prediction_region.constrained_decoding
Frame-independent models (Random Forest, etc.) ignore it entirely
The MLModelOperation base class default delegates to predictSequences()

