Using extractParameterSchema
The ParameterSchema dependency enables extractParameterSchema&lt;T&gt;() on all per-component param structs:
#include "channel_decoding/DecoderParamSchemas.hpp"

auto schema = WhiskerToolbox::Transforms::V2::extractParameterSchema&lt;dl::MaskDecoderParams&gt;();
// schema.fields[0].name == "threshold"
// schema.fields[0].type_name == "float"
// schema.fields[0].tooltip == "Binary threshold for mask generation"
For encoder params, RasterMode is automatically detected as an enum type:
#include "channel_encoding/EncoderParamSchemas.hpp"

auto schema = WhiskerToolbox::Transforms::V2::extractParameterSchema&lt;dl::Point2DEncoderParams&gt;();
// schema.fields[0].name == "mode"
// schema.fields[0].type_name == "enum"
// schema.fields[0].allowed_values == {"Binary", "Heatmap"}  (restricted by ParameterUIHints)

ParameterUIHints Pattern
Following the ProcessingOptionsSchema.hpp pattern used by MediaProcessingPipeline, UI annotations are kept in companion headers:
// channel_decoding/DecoderParamSchemas.hpp
template&lt;&gt;
struct ParameterUIHints&lt;dl::MaskDecoderParams&gt; {
    static void annotate(ParameterSchema & schema) {
        if (auto * f = schema.field("threshold")) {
            f-&gt;tooltip = "Binary threshold for mask generation";
            f-&gt;min_value = 0.01;
            f-&gt;max_value = 1.0;
            f-&gt;is_exclusive_min = true;
        }
    }
};
These hints are automatically applied when extractParameterSchema&lt;T&gt;() is called, since the template function invokes ParameterUIHints&lt;T&gt;::annotate(schema) as its final step. Include the appropriate *ParamSchemas.hpp header to ensure the specialization is visible at the point of schema extraction.

Usage

Option 1: C++ GeneralEncoderModel (Compiled)
The default constructor creates a model expecting 3×224×224 RGB input and 384×7×7 output (matching ConvNeXt-Tiny):
auto model = std::make_unique&lt;dl::GeneralEncoderModel&gt;();
model-&gt;loadWeights("/path/to/convnext_tiny_encoder.pt");

// Prepare input
auto image = torch::randn({1, 3, 224, 224});
std::unordered_map&lt;std::string, torch::Tensor&gt; inputs;
inputs["image"] = image;

// Run inference
auto outputs = model-&gt;forward(inputs);
auto features = outputs["features"];  // [1, 384, 7, 7]
For a different backbone, pass custom dimensions:
// ConvNeXt-Base: 1024-dim features
auto model = std::make_unique&lt;dl::GeneralEncoderModel&gt;(
    3, 224, 224, std::vector&lt;int64_t&gt;{1024, 7, 7});


Option 2: RuntimeModelSpec JSON (No Recompilation)
Create a JSON spec file (see examples/convnext_encoder_spec.json):
{
  "model_id": "convnext_tiny_encoder",
  "display_name": "ConvNeXt-Tiny Encoder",
  "weights_path": "convnext_tiny_encoder.pt",
  "batch_mode": { "dynamic": { "min": 1, "max": 32 } },
  "inputs": [
    { "name": "image", "shape": [3, 224, 224], "recommended_encoder": "ImageEncoder" }
  ],
  "outputs": [
    { "name": "features", "shape": [768, 7, 7] }
  ]
}
Load it via the ModelRegistry:
dl::ModelRegistry::instance().loadFromJson("/path/to/spec.json");
auto model = dl::ModelRegistry::instance().create("convnext_tiny_encoder");


Option 3: Widget UI
The GeneralEncoderModel appears in the Deep Learning widget’s model selector as “General Encoder”. Select it, configure the image binding, load weights, and run inference through the standard widget workflow.

Runtime Shape Configuration
Different encoder backbones produce different output shapes and may require different input resolutions. For example:

ConvNeXt-Tiny: 224×224 input → 384×7×7 output
EfficientViT: 256×256 input → 192×16×16 output
ViT-Base: 224×224 input → 768×14×14 output

The widget provides an Encoder Shape section (visible when “General Encoder” is selected) with spinboxes for Input Height, Input Width, and a text field for Output Shape. Set these to match your compiled model before loading weights.






Important



AOT Inductor models compiled with fixed spatial dimensions will produce NaN output if fed the wrong input size. Always match the input resolution to what was used during torch.export.export(). When using torch.export.Dim() for dynamic shapes, only the batch dimension is typically dynamic — spatial dimensions remain fixed.




Programmatic Shape Configuration
From C++, use the setter methods after construction:
auto model = std::make_unique&lt;dl::GeneralEncoderModel&gt;();
model-&gt;setInputResolution(256, 256);
model-&gt;setOutputShape({192, 16, 16});
model-&gt;loadWeights("/path/to/efficientvit_encoder.pt2");
The SlotAssembler also exposes configureModelShape() for widget-level access:
assembler-&gt;configureModelShape(256, 256, {192, 16, 16});

BatchMode
Every ModelBase subclass reports its batch-size constraints via batchMode(). The return type is dl::BatchMode, a std::variant with three alternatives:








Mode
Meaning
Example




FixedBatch{N}
Model is compiled for exactly N samples per call
AOT-compiled .pt2 with fixed shapes


DynamicBatch{min, max}
Model accepts a range of batch sizes (max=0 → unlimited)
TorchScript model with dynamic shapes


RecurrentOnlyBatch{}
Batch size is locked to 1 (sequential feedback loop)
NeuroSAM




C++ API
// In your ModelBase subclass:
dl::BatchMode batchMode() const override {
    return dl::RecurrentOnlyBatch{};   // or FixedBatch{4}, DynamicBatch{1, 8}
}

// Querying:
auto mode = model-&gt;batchMode();
bool locked = dl::isBatchLocked(mode);          // true for RecurrentOnly or Fixed(1)
int  max    = dl::maxBatchSizeFromMode(mode);   // 0 = unlimited
int  min    = dl::minBatchSizeFromMode(mode);
auto desc   = dl::batchModeDescription(mode);   // "RecurrentOnly", "Fixed(4)", etc.


JSON Specification (RuntimeModelSpec)
Runtime-defined models specify batch mode in their JSON spec:
{
  "model_id": "my_model",
  "display_name": "My Model",
  "batch_mode": { "fixed": 4 },
  "inputs": [...],
  "outputs": [...]
}
Supported formats:

{ "fixed": N } — Fixed batch size
{ "dynamic": { "min": 1, "max": 8 } } — Dynamic range (max=0 for unlimited)
{ "recurrent_only": true } — Recurrent-only

If batch_mode is omitted, the legacy preferred_batch_size / max_batch_size fields are used to construct a DynamicBatch.

