Location: src/DataSynthesizer/Trajectory/Trajectory.hpp + Trajectory.cpp

TrajectoryParams
struct TrajectoryParams {
    std::string model = "linear";       // "linear", "sinusoidal", "brownian"

    // Linear
    float velocity_x = 1.0f;
    float velocity_y = 0.0f;

    // Sinusoidal
    float amplitude_x = 0.0f;
    float amplitude_y = 0.0f;
    float frequency_x = 0.0f;
    float frequency_y = 0.0f;
    float phase_x = 0.0f;
    float phase_y = 0.0f;

    // Brownian
    float diffusion = 1.0f;
    uint64_t seed = 42;

    // Boundary
    std::string boundary_mode = "clamp"; // "clamp", "bounce", "wrap"
    float bounds_min_x = 0.0f;
    float bounds_max_x = 640.0f;
    float bounds_min_y = 0.0f;
    float bounds_max_y = 480.0f;
};
TrajectoryParams is used internally by computeTrajectory(). It is not used directly for JSON parsing — generators use typed parameter structs with MotionModelVariant (an rfl::TaggedUnion) and BoundaryParams (with BoundaryMode enum), then convert to TrajectoryParams via toTrajectoryParams().


computeTrajectory()
std::vector&lt;Point2D&lt;float&gt;&gt; computeTrajectory(
    float start_x, float start_y,
    int num_frames,
    TrajectoryParams const & params);
Returns num_frames positions starting from (start_x, start_y). Frame 0 is always the start position. Boundary enforcement is applied per-step.


Motion Models








Model
Formula
Key Parameters




linear
pos(t) = start + velocity × t
velocity_x, velocity_y


sinusoidal
pos(t) = start + amplitude × sin(2π × frequency × t + phase)
amplitude_*, frequency_*, phase_*


brownian
pos(t) = pos(t-1) + N(0, diffusion)
diffusion, seed





Boundary Modes
Boundaries are enforced per-step on each axis independently.







Mode
Behavior




clamp
Position is clamped to [min, max]. Velocity unchanged.


bounce
Position reflects off boundaries. Velocity is negated on reflection (linear model).


wrap
Position wraps around: if pos &gt; max, pos -= range; if pos &lt; min, pos += range.

Typed Motion Parameter System (Milestone 5b-ia)
Location: src/DataSynthesizer/Trajectory/MotionParams.hpp
Milestone 5b-ia replaced the flat-optional parameter style with a typed system using enum class for boundary modes and rfl::TaggedUnion for motion model selection. This integrates with ParameterSchema and AutoParamWidget to provide:

A combo box for selecting motion model (linear / sinusoidal / brownian)
A swapping sub-form showing only the relevant parameters for the selected model
A combo box for selecting boundary mode (clamp / bounce / wrap)


Shared Types
All moving generators reuse these types from MotionParams.hpp:
// Boundary
enum class BoundaryMode { clamp, bounce, wrap };

struct BoundaryParams {
    BoundaryMode boundary_mode = BoundaryMode::clamp;
    float bounds_min_x = 0.0f;
    float bounds_max_x = 640.0f;
    float bounds_min_y = 0.0f;
    float bounds_max_y = 480.0f;
};

// Per-model params
struct LinearMotionParams {
    float velocity_x = 1.0f;
    float velocity_y = 0.0f;
};

struct SinusoidalMotionParams {
    float amplitude_x = 0.0f;
    float amplitude_y = 0.0f;
    float frequency_x = 0.0f;
    float frequency_y = 0.0f;
    float phase_x = 0.0f;
    float phase_y = 0.0f;
};

struct BrownianMotionParams {
    float diffusion = 1.0f;
    uint64_t seed = 42;
};

// Discriminated union
using MotionModelVariant = rfl::TaggedUnion&lt;
    "model",
    LinearMotionParams,
    SinusoidalMotionParams,
    BrownianMotionParams&gt;;


toTrajectoryParams()
Converts from the typed variant + boundary params to the internal TrajectoryParams used by computeTrajectory():
TrajectoryParams toTrajectoryParams(
    MotionModelVariant const & motion,
    BoundaryParams const & boundary);
Uses motion.visit() to dispatch on the active variant alternative and populate the corresponding TrajectoryParams fields.


DefaultIfMissing
The RegisterGenerator template uses rfl::DefaultIfMissing as a processor when parsing JSON, so missing fields in the JSON use the struct’s default member initializer values. This means users only need to specify the fields they want to override.

Example: Writing a Generator
// src/DataSynthesizer/Generators/Analog/SineWaveGenerator.cpp

#include "DataSynthesizer/Registration.hpp"
#include "AnalogTimeSeries/Analog_Time_Series.hpp"

struct SineWaveParams {
    int num_samples = 1000;
    float amplitude = 1.0f;
    float frequency = 0.01f;
    float phase = 0.0f;
    float dc_offset = 0.0f;
};

static DataTypeVariant generateSineWave(SineWaveParams const & params) {
    auto ts = std::make_shared&lt;AnalogTimeSeries&gt;();
    std::vector&lt;float&gt; data(params.num_samples);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; params.num_samples; ++i) {
        data[i] = params.dc_offset +
                  params.amplitude * std::sin(2.0f * M_PI * params.frequency * i + params.phase);
    }
    ts-&gt;setData(data);
    return ts;
}

namespace {
auto const reg = WhiskerToolbox::DataSynthesizer::RegisterGenerator&lt;SineWaveParams&gt;(
    "SineWave",
    generateSineWave,
    WhiskerToolbox::DataSynthesizer::GeneratorMetadata{
        .description = "Generate a sine wave signal",
        .category = "Periodic",
        .output_type = "AnalogTimeSeries"
    });
}

Fix Patterns
Three complementary strategies address the problem at different layers. They can be adopted independently and incrementally.

Pattern 1: Deferred Writes (Worker-Side)
Idea: The background worker never calls DataManager::setData(). Instead, it stores its output in the result struct. The main-thread completion handler writes to DataManager after the worker finishes.
When to use: Any QThread worker that produces data to write into DataManager (pipelines, inference, batch transforms).
Already implemented: MLCoreWidget for both ClassificationPipeline and ClusteringPipeline.

How It Works
         Background Thread                 Main Thread
         ────────────────                  ───────────
         run pipeline logic
         store output in result struct
         emit finished()
                                    ← Qt::QueuedConnection ←
                                           _onPipelineComplete()
                                           dm.setData(...)
                                           notifyObservers() [safe: main thread]


Step-by-Step: Adding Deferred Writes to a New Worker

Add defer_dm_writes to the pipeline config struct:

struct MyPipelineConfig {
    // ... existing fields ...

    /// When true, the pipeline stores output in the result struct
    /// instead of writing to DataManager. The caller is responsible
    /// for writing the deferred output on the main thread.
    bool defer_dm_writes = false;
};

Add deferred output fields to the result struct:

struct MyPipelineResult {
    bool success = false;
    std::string error_message;

    // ... existing fields ...

    /// Deferred output — populated when defer_dm_writes is true.
    /// The caller must write this to DataManager on the main thread.
    std::optional&lt;OutputType&gt; deferred_output;
};

Conditionally skip writes in the pipeline:

if (config.defer_dm_writes) {
    result.deferred_output = std::move(output);
} else {
    writeOutput(dm, output);  // existing code path for tests/scripts
}

Write in the main-thread completion handler:

void MyWidget::_onWorkerComplete() {
    // This runs on the main thread (Qt::QueuedConnection from finished())
    if (_last_result-&gt;deferred_output.has_value()) {
        writeOutput(*_data_manager, *_last_result-&gt;deferred_output);
        _last_result-&gt;deferred_output.reset();
    }
}

Set defer_dm_writes = true in the widget’s config builder:

auto config = _buildConfig();
config.defer_dm_writes = true;  // Widget always defers






Note



Default defer_dm_writes to false so that non-GUI callers (unit tests, scripts, CLI tools) continue working without changes.





Pattern 2: Thread-Safe Callback Wrappers (Observer-Side)
Idea: Each widget wraps its observer callback so that Qt widget operations always execute on the main thread, regardless of which thread calls notifyObservers().
When to use: Any widget that registers an observer callback on DataManager or any ObserverData-based object. Use this to harden existing callbacks without modifying the data-producing code.

Option A: QTimer::singleShot(0, ...)
Defers the callback body to the next main-thread event loop iteration.
// BEFORE (unsafe — runs on calling thread):
auto id = dm-&gt;addObserver([this]() {
    _combo_box-&gt;clear();
    for (auto const & key : dm-&gt;getKeys()) {
        _combo_box-&gt;addItem(QString::fromStdString(key));
    }
});

// AFTER (safe — always runs on main thread):
auto id = dm-&gt;addObserver([this]() {
    QTimer::singleShot(0, this, [this]() {
        _combo_box-&gt;clear();
        for (auto const & key : dm-&gt;getKeys()) {
            _combo_box-&gt;addItem(QString::fromStdString(key));
        }
    });
});
Pros: Simple, one-line wrapper. The this receiver ensures the lambda is not invoked if the widget is destroyed before the timer fires.
Cons: Introduces a one-event-loop-iteration delay. Multiple observers may see a stale DataManager state between iterations.


Option B: QMetaObject::invokeMethod with Qt::QueuedConnection
Equivalent to QTimer::singleShot(0, ...) but more explicit about intent:
auto id = dm-&gt;addObserver([this]() {
    QMetaObject::invokeMethod(this, [this]() {
        refreshList();
    }, Qt::QueuedConnection);
});
Pros: Explicit connection type. Familiar to Qt developers.
Cons: Slightly more verbose.


Step-by-Step: Hardening an Existing Callback

Find the addObserver call in the widget’s constructor or setup method.
Identify the callback body — the lambda passed to addObserver.
Wrap the body with QTimer::singleShot(0, this, [this]() { ... }).
Verify the widget stores the CallbackID and calls removeObserver in its destructor. (This is an existing requirement, not new.)
Test: Run the widget with ASAN/TSAN enabled while triggering observer notifications from a background thread.




Pattern 3: Main-Thread Assertion (Defensive)
Idea: Add a debug-only assertion to ObserverData::notifyObservers() that verifies it is being called from the Qt main thread. This catches violations at the call site rather than waiting for a crash downstream.
When to use: As a global safety net. Once added, every existing and future notifyObservers() call is automatically guarded.

Implementation
Add to Observer_Data.cpp:
#include &lt;cassert&gt;

#ifdef QT_CORE_LIB
#include &lt;QThread&gt;
#include &lt;QCoreApplication&gt;
#endif

void ObserverData::notifyObservers() {
#if !defined(NDEBUG) && defined(QT_CORE_LIB)
    // In debug builds with Qt available, assert we're on the main thread.
    // ObserverData callbacks typically manipulate Qt widgets, which require
    // the main thread. If this fires, the caller must defer the write to
    // the main thread (see observer_thread_safety_roadmap.qmd).
    auto const * app = QCoreApplication::instance();
    if (app != nullptr) {
        assert(QThread::currentThread() == app-&gt;thread()
               && "notifyObservers() called from non-main thread. "
                  "Observer callbacks may touch Qt widgets. "
                  "Use deferred writes or QTimer::singleShot to marshal to main thread.");
    }
#endif

    for (auto const & [id, callback] : _observers) {
        callback();
    }
}






Warning



Adding this assertion will immediately surface all existing violations in debug builds. Before enabling it project-wide, either (a) harden all unsafe callbacks first, or (b) add it behind a #define that can be enabled per-developer for auditing.




Staged Rollout for the Assertion

Phase A — Opt-in: Add the assertion behind #ifdef WHISKER_ASSERT_OBSERVER_MAIN_THREAD. Developers enable it locally to find violations.
Phase B — Fix callbacks: Work through the unsafe callback list above, wrapping each with QTimer::singleShot. This can be done incrementally.
Phase C — Enable by default: Remove the opt-in #ifdef and enable the assertion unconditionally in debug builds. Any new violation is caught immediately during development.



Alternative: ASSERT_MAIN_THREAD Macro
For use in individual widget callbacks:
#if !defined(NDEBUG) && defined(QT_CORE_LIB)
#define ASSERT_MAIN_THREAD() \
    do { \
        auto const * _app = QCoreApplication::instance(); \
        assert((!_app || QThread::currentThread() == _app-&gt;thread()) \
               && "Must be called from the main thread"); \
    } while (false)
#else
#define ASSERT_MAIN_THREAD() ((void)0)
#endif
Usage in a widget method called from an observer:
void MyWidget::refreshList() {
    ASSERT_MAIN_THREAD();
    _combo_box-&gt;clear();
    // ...
}

