KeyActionAdapter (composition)
Widgets that want to receive keymap dispatches create a KeyActionAdapter as a child QObject. This uses composition over inheritance — no multiple inheritance or virtual interfaces required.
class KeyActionAdapter : public QObject {
    Q_OBJECT
public:
    using Handler = std::function&lt;bool(QString const &)&gt;;

    explicit KeyActionAdapter(QObject * parent = nullptr);

    /// Set the handler called when an action is dispatched to this adapter.
    void setHandler(Handler handler);

    /// Called by KeymapManager. Delegates to the installed handler.
    bool handleKeyAction(QString const & action_id);

    /// The EditorInstanceId this adapter is associated with.
    void setInstanceId(EditorInstanceId const & id);
    [[nodiscard]] EditorInstanceId instanceId() const;
};
When a widget is created, it constructs a KeyActionAdapter as a child and registers it with KeymapManager. When the widget is destroyed, Qt’s parent-child ownership automatically destroys the adapter, and KeymapManager detects this via QObject::destroyed to clean up its routing table.
This approach avoids the diamond-inheritance problems that arise when multiple mixins are combined with QObject-derived classes.



KeymapManager
Central singleton-like object (owned by MainWindow). Responsibilities:

Action registry — Stores all KeyActionDescriptors. Widgets register descriptors during registerTypes().
Event filter — Installed on QApplication. Intercepts QEvent::KeyPress, applies scope resolution, and dispatches to the correct handler.
Override management — Merges shipped defaults with user customizations. Detects and reports conflicts (same key bound to multiple actions in the same scope).
Persistence — Serializes user overrides to AppPreferencesData (stored in ~/.config/WhiskerToolbox/preferences.json).
Auto-generated focus actions — For every registered EditorTypeId, creates a "focus.&lt;type_id&gt;" action (default: unbound) that raises the dock widget and transfers focus.



Dispatch Flow
QKeyEvent arrives at QApplication event filter
  │
  ├── Is focused widget a text input? → pass through to Qt
  │
  ├── Look up focused editor via SelectionContext::activeEditorId()
  │   └── Find EditorFocused actions for that type matching this key
  │       └── Found? → look up KeyActionAdapter for that editor instance
  │           └── Call adapter-&gt;handleKeyAction(action_id) → done
  │
  ├── Find AlwaysRouted actions matching this key
  │   └── Found? → look up KeyActionAdapter for the target editor type
  │       └── Call adapter-&gt;handleKeyAction(action_id) → done
  │
  ├── Find Global actions matching this key
  │   └── Found? → execute (focus switch, app command, etc.) → done
  │
  └── No match → pass through to Qt (event not consumed)


Registration Pattern
Widgets register action descriptors (pure metadata) during their registerTypes() call. No callbacks or widget pointers are stored at registration time.
At runtime, when a widget instance is created, it constructs a KeyActionAdapter, installs a handler, and registers the adapter with KeymapManager. Dispatch happens through the adapter — no virtual interface inheritance required.
// During type registration (pure metadata, no widget instances yet):
keymap_manager-&gt;registerAction({
    .action_id = "media.assign_group_1",
    .display_name = "Assign to Group 1",
    .category = "Media Viewer",
    .scope = KeyActionScope::EditorFocused(EditorTypeId("MediaWidget")),
    .default_binding = QKeySequence(Qt::Key_1)
});
// ... repeat for groups 2–9
When a Media_Window is created, it sets up its adapter:
// In Media_Window constructor:
_key_adapter = new KeyActionAdapter(this);  // QObject parenting
_key_adapter-&gt;setInstanceId(_state-&gt;getInstanceId());
_key_adapter-&gt;setHandler([this](QString const & action_id) -&gt; bool {
    if (action_id == "media.assign_group_1") {
        assignToGroup(1);
        return true;
    }
    // ...
    return false;
});
keymap_manager-&gt;registerAdapter(_key_adapter);
// Deregistration is automatic via QObject::destroyed signal


Persistence
User keybinding overrides are stored in AppPreferencesData:
struct KeymapOverrideEntry {
    std::string action_id;        // "timeline.play_pause"
    std::string key_sequence;     // "Space"
    bool enabled = true;
};

struct AppPreferencesData {
    // ... existing fields ...
    std::vector&lt;KeymapOverrideEntry&gt; keybinding_overrides;
};
This is app-wide (not per-workspace) — the same keybindings apply everywhere, matching Blender’s model. Serialized via reflect-cpp to ~/.config/WhiskerToolbox/preferences.json.


Keybinding Editor Widget
A dedicated editor (registered via EditorRegistry, opened from the View menu) that displays all actions grouped by category:

Each row shows: action name, scope badge, current key binding, and a “Record” button
Pressing “Record” then pressing a key captures the new binding
Conflicting bindings (same key + same scope) are highlighted
“Reset to Default” per-action and “Reset All” buttons
Changes are persisted on apply

Decision taken: Option B.
What was done: - ProcessingOptions.hpp kept as plain structs with no rfl dependency. - ParameterUIHints specializations for all 8 structs created in src/MediaProcessingPipeline/ProcessingOptionsSchema.hpp (part of the Phase 3 library). - ColormapType enum handled automatically by reflect-cpp’s enumerator array support. - Non-UI fields excluded via Phase 2 struct split (see below).
Original tasks (for reference):

Add rfl::Validator constraints to struct fields to encode UI ranges. For example:
struct GammaOptions {
    bool active{false};
    rfl::Validator&lt;double, rfl::Minimum&lt;0&gt;, rfl::Maximum&lt;10&gt;&gt; gamma{1.0};
};
Add ParameterUIHints specializations for each struct to provide display names, tooltips, and field grouping. These live in a separate header so ImageProcessing doesn’t need an rfl dependency — the hints can live in a new bridge header.
Handle ColormapType enum — this already works with reflect-cpp’s rfl::get_enumerator_array&lt;T&gt;() since it’s a plain enum class. AutoParamWidget will generate a QComboBox automatically.
Exclude non-UI fields — MagicEraserOptions::mask, MagicEraserOptions::image_size, and MagicEraserOptions::drawing_mode are runtime-only state, not user-editable parameters. These need to be split out (see Phase 2).




The ProcessingOptions.hpp structs currently live in ImageProcessing, which has no rfl dependency.
Option A: Add rfl dependency to ImageProcessing (simplest, but heavier).
Option B (recommended): Keep the plain structs in ImageProcessing as-is. Create a companion header (e.g., ProcessingOptionsSchema.hpp) in the new library (Phase 3) that provides extractParameterSchema specializations and ParameterUIHints for each struct. This requires the structs to be simple aggregates, which they already are (except ContrastOptions with its methods — those can remain since reflect-cpp handles method-bearing structs fine as long as the fields are public).

What was done: - Created src/MediaProcessingPipeline/ with: - ProcessingOptionsSchema.hpp — ParameterUIHints specializations for all 8 option structs. - ProcessingStep.hpp — descriptor struct (display_name, chain_key, schema, apply). - ProcessingStepRegistry.hpp/.cpp — singleton registry with RegisterStep&lt;T&gt; RAII helper. - CMakeLists.txt — static library aliased as WhiskerToolbox::MediaProcessingPipeline. - 6 steps statically registered: Linear Transform, Gamma Correction, Image Sharpening, CLAHE, Bilateral Filter, Median Filter. Magic Eraser and Colormap are handled separately (state/rendering requirements). - Wired into src/CMakeLists.txt after DataSynthesizer. - Developer docs created at docs/developer/MediaProcessingPipeline/index.qmd.
