Model Registry
MLModelRegistry (models/MLModelRegistry.hpp) is a factory registry that stores factory functions for ML models. Each create() call returns a fresh, untrained model instance.

Built-in Registration
The constructor registers all six built-in models. External code can register additional models via registerModel().


API
MLModelRegistry registry;

// Query
auto all_names = registry.getAllModelNames();
auto classifiers = registry.getModelNames(MLTaskType::MultiClassClassification);
auto clusterers = registry.getModelNames(MLTaskType::Clustering);
bool has_rf = registry.hasModel("Random Forest");

// Create
auto model = registry.create("Random Forest");  // fresh, untrained instance
auto params = model-&gt;getDefaultParameters();

// Register custom model
registry.registerModel&lt;MyCustomModel&gt;();
// or:
registry.registerModel("My Model", MLTaskType::Clustering, []() {
    return std::make_unique&lt;MyCustomModel&gt;();
});


Custom Model Registration
To add a new model:

Subclass MLModelOperation and implement the relevant methods
Create a corresponding parameter struct inheriting MLModelParametersBase
Register via registerModel&lt;T&gt;() or the three-argument overload

class MyModel : public MLModelOperation {
public:
    std::string getName() const override { return "My Model"; }
    MLTaskType getTaskType() const override { return MLTaskType::Clustering; }
    std::unique_ptr&lt;MLModelParametersBase&gt; getDefaultParameters() const override {
        return std::make_unique&lt;MyModelParameters&gt;();
    }
    bool fit(arma::mat const & features, MLModelParametersBase const * params) override;
    bool assignClusters(arma::mat const & features, arma::Row&lt;size_t&gt; & assignments) override;
    bool isTrained() const override { return _trained; }
private:
    bool _trained = false;
};

// In the registry constructor or your registration code:
registry.registerModel&lt;MyModel&gt;();

Classification Pipeline
ClassificationPipeline (pipelines/ClassificationPipeline.hpp) orchestrates supervised classification end-to-end.

Pipeline Stages
For frame-based models (Random Forest, Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression):
ValidatingFeatures → ConvertingFeatures → AssemblingLabels →
BalancingClasses → Training → Predicting → ComputingMetrics → WritingOutput → Complete
For sequence models (HMM):
ValidatingFeatures → ConvertingFeatures → AssemblingLabels →
BalancingClasses → SegmentingSequences → Training → Predicting →
ComputingMetrics → WritingOutput → Complete
The SegmentingSequences stage uses SequenceAssembler::segment() to split the feature matrix and labels into contiguous temporal segments (based on gaps in TimeFrameIndex). Training then routes through trainSequences() and prediction through predictSequences(), preserving temporal structure for models like HMMs. When constrained_decoding is enabled and training labels are available, prediction uses predictSequencesConstrained() to clamp the HMM’s initial distribution at segment boundaries where the ground truth state is known.


Configuration
ClassificationPipelineConfig config;

// Model
config.model_name = "Random Forest";
config.model_params = &rf_params;  // or nullptr for defaults

// Features
config.feature_tensor_key = "ml_features";

// Labels (variant — pick one mode)
config.label_config = LabelFromIntervals{"Contact", "No Contact"};
config.label_interval_key = "contact_intervals";

// Conversion
config.conversion_config.zscore_normalize = true;

// Balancing (optional)
config.balance_classes = true;
config.balancing_config.strategy = BalancingStrategy::Subsample;

// Prediction region
config.prediction_region.predict_all_rows = true;
// Optional: filter output to a specific interval (runs Viterbi on full span
// for temporal context, writes only frames within the interval)
// config.prediction_region.prediction_interval_key = "target_region";
// config.prediction_region.constrained_decoding = true; // HMM warm-start

// Output
